    "description": "The Persian onager is an Iranian subspecies of Asiatic wild ass adapted to semi-desert landscapes. Its conservation requires space for movement, access to vegetation and water, and careful separation of surviving native populations from reintroduced groups.",
    "tags": ["Persian onager", "Asiatic wild ass", "Dryland ecology", "Wildlife reintroduction", "Habitat connectivity"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T12:24:51Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T12:24:51Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Persian Onager: Iran’s Wild Ass and Its Dryland Habitat | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Discover the Persian onager’s ecology, native strongholds, road and farming conflicts, and evidence from reintroduction research in Kavir National Park."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "wild-equid",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A wild equid of semi-desert country",
        "content": "The Persian onager, *Equus hemionus onager*, belongs to the horse family and is a subspecies of Asiatic wild ass. It has a sandy coat, an upright mane, and a dark stripe along its back. Its appearance may recall a donkey, but this page concerns a wild animal and its conservation in Iranian landscapes. [s1]\n\nOnagers feed on grasses, herbs, shrubs, and foliage. Their dryland adaptation does not eliminate dependence on water: access to water sources and changing forage conditions remains important. The Smithsonian’s species account describes territorial males and females moving between territories, alongside variation in social grouping. This is a useful reminder that a photographed herd does not represent a permanently fixed social unit. [s1]\n\nMovement is central to their ecology. Seasonal and irregular changes in resources make access to a wider landscape important, especially during environmental extremes. Protecting an isolated patch of suitable vegetation cannot necessarily meet those changing requirements. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "native-and-reintroduced",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Native strongholds and restored populations",
        "content": "A study published in March 2026 distinguishes Iran’s surviving indigenous populations from reintroduced groups. It places the southern indigenous population primarily in Qatrouiyeh National Park within the Bahram-e-Goor Protected Area, and the central-northern population in Touran Biosphere Reserve. The authors also identify reintroduced groups in Kalmand Protected Area and Siah-Kooh National Park. These distinctions matter when interpreting a distribution map: presence following a release is different evidence from uninterrupted persistence. [s2]\n\nThe study evaluated reintroduction in Kavir National Park using field surveys and demographic records. Onager occupancy was associated with particular shrub communities and proximity to ranger stations. The researchers also identified substantial early mortality associated with translocation stress and social aggression in the acclimatisation site. Their findings support adapting group composition, release preparation, and subsequent monitoring rather than equating the act of release with establishment of a secure population. These are findings from Kavir, not a nationwide estimate of reintroduction success. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "roads-and-farming",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Where conservation meets roads and farms",
        "content": "Research published in Scientific Reports in 2021 combined community questionnaires with spatial analysis in southern Iran. The authors examined attitudes and crop damage alongside modeled habitat connections and recorded road crossings and collisions. Their results identified roads cutting through core movement areas, with collisions concentrated in affected sections. A protected landscape can therefore remain hazardous when transport routes interrupt movement within it. [s3]\n\nThe study also found an association between greater ecological knowledge among local communities and less reported crop damage than among those relying on traditional deterrent methods. This observational result does not establish that information alone prevents conflict. The authors proposed combining habitat and corridor protection, collision mitigation, and mediation with communities. Their approach treats farmers’ experiences as part of the conservation problem rather than assuming that designation of a reserve removes competing interests. [s3]\n\nTaken together, the research points toward a landscape-based definition of recovery: animals must survive, reproduce, and retain access to the resources they need. Reintroduction and protected-area management contribute to that goal, but their outcomes require monitoring. The evidence supports this conservation framework more securely than it supports a single current national population figure. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
